course: books
lesson: 82
title: Just So Stories, The Beginning of the Armadillos, part 1: Painted Jaguar and the muddled advice
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On the banks of the muddy Amazon live two friends: Stickly-Prickly Hedgehog, who eats snails, and Slow-Solid Tortoise, who eats lettuces. Painted Jaguar lives there too, and his Mother has told him how to eat them: drop a Hedgehog in the water so he uncoils, and scoop a Tortoise out of his shell with your paw.
explain: One night Jaguar finds the two under a fallen tree. Hedgehog curls into a ball and Tortoise pulls in his head and feet, so Jaguar cannot tell which is which. The two friends tease him by saying his Mother's advice back to him all jumbled up, until his head spins.
explain: Hedgehog says «you can scoop me out of my shell», so Jaguar thinks he must be Tortoise and grabs him. His paw fills with prickles, and Hedgehog flies off into the dark bushes. Tortoise then talks in such knots that Jaguar orders him into the river, which is just what Tortoise wanted: he dives and swims away.
explain: Jaguar roars for his Mother. She explains clearly: a Hedgehog curls up and his prickles stick out; a Tortoise cannot curl up, he only pulls in his head and legs. The friends, listening from under a leaf, are worried: even Jaguar cannot forget such clear directions.
fact: This is the seventh of Kipling's twelve Just So Stories (1902); it begins again with «O Best Beloved» and «the High and Far-Off Times», like the Elephant's Child.
fact: Painted Jaguar eats everything he can catch; when he cannot catch deer or monkeys, he eats frogs and beetles.
fact: «Turbid» means muddy and cloudy; Kipling calls the Amazon «the turbid Amazon» again and again.
fact: Mother Jaguar's rule: «when you find a Hedgehog you must drop him into the water and then he will uncoil, and when you catch a Tortoise you must scoop him out of his shell with your paw».
fact: Hedgehog and Tortoise answer with the same words shuffled: «when you water a Hedgehog you must drop him into your paw». The words are real but the sense is nonsense, and that is the joke.
fact: Jaguar says «to save my spots» and «You are making my spots ache»; in old English «paddy-paw» is a baby word for paw.
fact: Hedgehog tricks Jaguar with «you can scoop me out of my shell if you like», so Jaguar guesses wrong, grabs him, and gets a paw full of prickles; Hedgehog is knocked far off into the dark woods and bushes, and Jaguar puts his paw into his mouth, which only makes the prickles hurt more.
fact: Tortoise ties Jaguar in knots of «she said I said» talk, then says «if you want to see me swim away you've only got to drop me into the water». Jaguar thinks it must be a trick, so he orders him into the river, and Tortoise swims away under water and comes out on the bank where Hedgehog is waiting for him.
fact: «I don't rib Painted Jaguar» is Kipling's playful spelling for «I don't like» or «I don't trust» him; «a very narrow escape» means they were nearly caught. Hiding under a leaf as Mother Jaguar explains, they say: «I don't like this old lady one little bit.»
fact: Mother Jaguar's clear rule: a Hedgehog «curls himself up into a ball and his prickles stick out every which way»; a Tortoise «can't curl himself up».
fact: Jaguar turns it into a rhyme: «Can't curl, but can swim, Slow-Solid, that's him! Curls up, but can't swim, Stickly-Prickly, that's him!»
fact: Real life: hedgehogs live in Europe, Asia and Africa, not by the Amazon in South America. Kipling put them there for the story.
fact: Real life: jaguars really live by the Amazon, love water and swim well, and their bite is strong enough to crack a turtle's shell, so a real tortoise would not be safe.
fact: Real life: a jaguar's spots are rosettes, rings with little dots inside; the leopard of Africa and Asia has rings with no dots. In Kipling's earlier tale the Leopard got his spots from a man's fingertips, which is made up too.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «lowkey» = a bit, secretly; «sus» = suspicious; «canny» (North East England) = clever, or quite; «y'all» (southern USA) = you lot.
exercise: Where do the Hedgehog, the Tortoise and the Jaguar live? | On the banks of the turbid Amazon | On the banks of the grey-green, greasy Limpopo | By the Red Sea, on an uninhabited island | On the High Veldt in South Africa
exercise: What does Stickly-Prickly Hedgehog eat? | Shelly snails and things | Green lettuces and things | Melons, thrown about rind and all | Frogs and beetles caught by the river
exercise: What does Slow-Solid Tortoise eat? | Green lettuces and things | Shelly snails and things | Cake-crumbs and burned currants | Sticks, thorns and prickles
exercise: When Painted Jaguar cannot catch deer or monkeys, what does he eat? | Frogs and beetles | Fish from the river | Grass and green leaves | Nothing at all, ever
exercise: What does Mother Jaguar tell him to do with a Hedgehog? | Drop him into the water so he uncoils | Scoop him out of his shell with a paw | Roll him down a hill until he opens up again | Leave him alone, as he is too prickly
exercise: And with a Tortoise? | Scoop him out of his shell with your paw | Drop him into the river so he uncurls | Turn him over and wait for the sun to dry him | Shake him till his head and feet fall out
exercise: Where does Jaguar find the two friends one beautiful night? | Under the trunk of a fallen tree | In a cave beside a waterfall | Up a tall palm-tree by the river | Asleep in a canoe
exercise: Why can't Jaguar tell which friend is which? | One is curled into a ball and the other has pulled into his shell | They look exactly alike, both being scaly all over like pine-cones | It is too dark to see them | They hide in the water
exercise: «Turbid» Amazon. What does turbid mean? | Muddy and cloudy | Very wide and slow | Fast and very loud | Warm and very shallow
exercise: How do Hedgehog and Tortoise answer Jaguar's question? | They say his Mother's advice back to him all mixed up | They tell him the truth at once | They run off as fast as they can into the trees | They call for Mother Jaguar
exercise: «You are making my spots ache.» What does Jaguar mean? | You are confusing me and giving me a headache | My spots are hurting from the cold | You scratched my spots | My spots are wearing off
exercise: Which friend says «you can scoop me out of my shell if you like»? | Stickly-Prickly Hedgehog | Slow-Solid Tortoise, from inside | Mother Jaguar | Painted Jaguar himself
exercise: What happens when Jaguar darts out his paw? | Hedgehog curls up and the paw fills with prickles | He scoops out the Tortoise and eats him for supper | He misses and falls into the river | The Hedgehog bites his nose
exercise: Where does Hedgehog end up after Jaguar's paw knocks him? | Far into the dark woods and bushes | Up in the top of a tall tree | In the river, swimming for his life | In Jaguar's mouth
exercise: How does Jaguar make his paw even worse? | He puts it into his mouth, and the prickles hurt him more | He rubs it against a palm-tree | He dips it in the cold river | He bangs it on a stone
exercise: What does Tortoise tell Jaguar he will do if he is dropped into the water? | Swim away | Drown at once | Turn into a fish | Curl into a ball
exercise: Why does Jaguar order Tortoise into the river? | He thinks Tortoise is so keen to be dropped that it must be a trick | His Mother told him to drop every animal | He wants Tortoise to fetch him a fish | He is thirsty and wants a drink
exercise: Where does Tortoise come out after swimming under water? | On the bank where Hedgehog is waiting | Inside Mother Jaguar's den | Far out at sea | On the other side of the world
exercise: What does Mother Jaguar say a Hedgehog does? | Curls up into a ball with prickles every which way | Swims under water for a very long way without coming up | Draws his head and legs into his shell | Climbs trees at night
exercise: What does she say about a Tortoise? | He can't curl up; he only draws his head and legs into his shell | He curls up tight with his prickles out | He can never swim, not even a little | He sleeps all winter in the mud
exercise: What do the two friends say about Mother Jaguar, hiding under a leaf? | «I don't like this old lady one little bit» | «What a kind and clever old lady she is, to be sure» | «She will never find us here» | «She has told him the rules all wrong»
exercise: Which rhyme does Jaguar say to himself at the end of this part? | «Can't curl, but can swim, Slow-Solid, that's him!» | «Humph yourself! Come out of the Desert and go to the Three, and behave» | «By means of a grating I have stopped your ating» | «Twinkle, twinkle, little bat»
exercise: Looking back: like this story, the Elephant's Child began in which times? | The High and Far-Off Times | The Time of the Very Beginnings | The days when everybody started fair | The Middle Ages
test: Jaz: «so Jaguar scooped the Tortoise out, ate him, end of story, lowkey sad.» (lowkey = a bit) Put it right. | No one is eaten: Jaguar grabs the Hedgehog by mistake and Tortoise swims off | Right, the Tortoise was eaten | Jaguar ate the Hedgehog, prickles and all | Mother Jaguar ate the Tortoise, not her son
test: Why does Hedgehog say «you can scoop me out of my shell»? | So Jaguar will think he is the Tortoise and grab the wrong animal | Because he has a hard shell under his prickles | Because he wants to be eaten first | Because he forgot which animal he was
test: Tortoise says: «if you want to see me swim away you've only got to drop me into the water.» Why is this clever? | It is true, but Jaguar thinks it is a trick and does exactly that | It is a lie that sends Jaguar off into the woods | It sends Jaguar to sleep | It frightens Mother Jaguar away
test: «That's sus,» says Tom, «a Jaguar drops the Tortoise in the river cos he WANTS to?» (sus = suspicious) What is the real reason? | Jaguar is so muddled he thinks the opposite of what Tortoise says must be true | Jaguar wants to watch the Tortoise race the fishes | Jaguar's Mother told him tortoises love water | Jaguar wanted to wash him first
test: What would happen if Jaguar had remembered his Mother's words exactly? | He would know the curled-up ball was Hedgehog and the shell was Tortoise | He would still be confused by his Mother's words | He would make friends with both | He would go home hungry anyway
test: Which is TRUE about this part of the story? | Jaguar never manages to eat either friend | Mother Jaguar gives muddled, mixed-up advice to her son | The Hedgehog swims away under water | Jaguar knows at once who is who
test: «Can't curl, but can swim, Slow-Solid, that's him!» Why are the friends worried by this rhyme? | It is so clear that even Jaguar will never forget it | It is a magic spell that Mother Jaguar uses against hedgehogs and tortoises | It tells Jaguar where they hide | It is rude about the Tortoise
test: Kai from Newcastle: «that Hedgehog's canny, like.» (canny = clever) Which trick shows it best? | Pretending to be the Tortoise, so Jaguar grabs his prickles | Building a little raft of sticks to float across the wide Amazon at night | Hiding in Mother Jaguar's den | Painting spots on himself
test: Real life: which statement is true? | Real jaguars swim well and can crack a turtle's shell with their bite | Real jaguars hate the water and never go near a river, even on the hottest day | Real hedgehogs live along the Amazon | Real tortoises roll into a ball
test: Looking back: in «How the Leopard Got His Spots», how did the Leopard get his spots? | A man pressed five fingertips on him | He rolled about in the stripy, speckly shadows of the forest until they stuck to his coat | A Djinn made them by magic | He was born with them, like all leopards
test: Looking back: Jaguar asks about his food and gets a paw full of prickles. Which earlier character asked what someone had for dinner and got a stretched nose? | The Elephant's Child, asking about the Crocodile | The Camel, asking the Djinn of All Deserts what he should eat on Thursday | Old Man Kangaroo, asking Nqong | The Whale, asking the 'Stute Fish
test: Looking back: in the Whale story, who gave the advice that got someone swallowed? | The small 'Stute Fish | Mother Jaguar, waving her tail | Kolokolo Bird in the bush | Baviaan the dog-headed Baboon
test: Looking back: the Elephant's Child asked many questions. Who sent him to the Limpopo to find out? | Kolokolo Bird | The Bi-Coloured-Python-Rock-Snake | The tall Ostrich | The old Crocodile
test: Real life, y'all (= you lot): where do hedgehogs really live? | In Europe, Asia and Africa | Only along the Amazon in South America | In Australia and nowhere else | Only in the sea, by the coast
test: ★★★ «When you scoop water with your paw you uncoil it with a Hedgehog» swaps the parts of the rule round. Which line is mixed up in the SAME way? | «When you fork a plate you dinner it with an eat» | «Put the Hedgehog gently in the river» | «Scoop the Tortoise slowly» | «Eat your lettuces, not your snails»
test: ★★★ «If she said what you said I said she said, it's just the same as if I said what she said she said.» How many times does the word «said» appear in that sentence? | Seven | Only five | Just six | Exactly eight
test: ★★★ Jaguar's own rhyme: «Can't curl, but can swim — Slow-Solid.» At this point in the story, which is true of the real Slow-Solid? | He cannot curl but can swim, so the rhyme is right | He can curl but cannot swim, so the rhyme is wrong | He can already do both things well | He can do neither
exam: Identify the instruction imparted by Mother Jaguar concerning the treatment of a Hedgehog. | That he be dropped into the water, whereupon he will uncoil | That he be scooped out of his shell by means of a paw, without delay | That he be left undisturbed on account of his prickles | That he be rolled along the river-bank until he opens
exam: It is given that Painted Jaguar inserted his paw into a curled-up animal. Ascertain the consequence. | His paw was filled with prickles and the animal was knocked into the bushes | The Tortoise was extracted from its shell and consumed forthwith, to the satisfaction of Mother Jaguar | The animal swam away beneath the river | Mother Jaguar reprimanded the Hedgehog
exam: Select the response most consistent with the established facts concerning the Tortoise's escape. | He induced Jaguar to order him into the river, and swam away beneath the water | He was carried off by the Hedgehog through the bushes, the Jaguar being occupied with his injured paw | He concealed himself beneath a leaf | He persuaded Mother Jaguar to release him
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the habitat of hedgehogs in the natural world? | Europe, Asia and Africa, and not the banks of the Amazon | Exclusively the banks of the Amazon and the adjacent forests of South America | The depths of the ocean | The polar regions only
